<p class="lt-chem-36383">After completing this section, you should be able to</p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-36383">write an equation for the oxidation of an aldehyde using
    <ul>
        <li class="lt-chem-36383">CrO<sub>3</sub>/sulphuric acid.</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-36383">Tollens reagent.</li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li class="lt-chem-36383">explain the difference in structure which makes aldehydes susceptible to oxidation and ketones difficult to oxidize.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-36383">identify the carboxylic acid produced when a given aldehyde is oxidized.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-36383">identify the aldehyde, the oxidizing agent, or both, needed to prepare a given carboxylic acid.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section"><span id="Key_Terms"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Key Terms</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-36383">Make certain that you can define, and use in context, the key term below.</p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-36383">Tollens reagent</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section"><span id="Study_Notes"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-36383 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Study Notes</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-36383">An important difference between aldehydes and ketones is the ease with which the latter can be oxidized. Tollen&rsquo;s reagent is a classical organic laboratory technique to test for the presence of an aldehyde. The reagent consists of silver(I) ions dissolved in dilute ammonia. When the aldehyde is oxidized, the silver(I) ions are reduced to silver metal. When the reaction is carried out in a test&#8209;tube, the metallic silver is deposited on the walls of the tube, giving it a mirrorlike appearance. This characteristic accounts for the term &ldquo;silver mirror test&rdquo; which is applied when this reaction is used to distinguish between aldehydes and ketones&mdash;the latter, of course, do not react.</p>
</div></section>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="Why_do_aldehydes_and_ketones_behave_differently.3F"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-36383 editable">Why do aldehydes and ketones behave differently?</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span>Aldehydes have a proton attached to the carbonyl carbon which can be abstracted, allowing them to be easily oxidized to form carboxylic acids. The lack of this hydrogen, makes ketones generally inert to these oxidation conditions. Nevertheless, ketones can be oxidized but only under extreme conditions.</span></p>

<figure><img alt="kekule structure of aldehyde and ketone, showing H on aldehyde carbon allows aldehydes to be oxidized.svg" style="width: 166px; height: 115px;" width="166px" height="115px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387959/kekule_structure_of_aldehyde_and_ketone%252C_showing_H_on_aldehyde_carbon_allows_aldehydes_to_be_oxidized.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=166&amp;height=115" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\): This characteristic accounts for the term &ldquo;silver mirror test&rdquo; which is applied when this reaction is used to distinguish between aldehydes and ketones&mdash;the latter, of course, do not react.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>A significant distinction between aldehydes and ketones lies in their susceptibility to oxidation, with aldehydes being more easily oxidized than ketones. One of the classic methods for identifying aldehydes in the laboratory is through the use of <strong>Tollen&rsquo;s reagent</strong>&nbsp;which&nbsp;consists of silver(I) ions dissolved in dilute ammonia. When an aldehyde is present, it undergoes oxidation, causing the silver(I) ions to be reduced to metallic silver. This reduction results in the deposition of silver on the surface of the test tube, forming a reflective, mirror-like coating. This reaction is commonly referred to as the &ldquo;silver mirror test,&rdquo; and it serves as a reliable way to distinguish aldehydes from ketones. Ketones, in contrast, do not react with Tollen's reagent, making this test specific for aldehydes.&nbsp;</p>

<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width: 342px; height: 277px;" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): Tollens test for Aldehyde. The left side is positive (silver mirror) and the right side is negative.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><img alt="Oxidation of hexanal to form hexanoic acid using Tollens Reagent.svg" style="width: 458px; height: 50px;" width="458px" height="50px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387958/Oxidation_of_hexanal_to_form_hexanoic%25C2%25A0acid_using_Tollens_Reagent.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=458&amp;height=50" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383">Oxidation of hexanal to form hexanoic acid using Tollens Reagent</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Oxidation_of_Aldehydes"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-36383 editable">Oxidation of Aldehydes</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span>There are a wide variety of reagents which can cause the oxidation of aldehydes to carboxylic acids. The most common reagent for this conversi</span>on is \(\ce{CrO3}\) in aq<span>ueous acid also called <strong>Jones Reagent</strong>. This reaction generally gives good yields at room temperature.</span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><img alt="Oxidation of hexanal to form hexanoic acid using Jones Reagent.svg" style="width: 435px; height: 55px;" width="435px" height="55px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387960/Oxidation_of_hexanal_to_form_hexanoic_acid_using_Jones_Reagent.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=435&amp;height=55" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383">Example of the oxidation of hexanal to form hexanoic acid using Jones Reagent</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="Mechanism"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-36383 editable">Mechanism</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span>The oxidation of aldehydes occur through the reversible nucleophilic addition of water to the carbonyl to form a gem-diol functional group. This addition reaction is discussed in greater detail in<a title="19.5: Nucleophilic Addition Reactions of Ketones and Aldehydes" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Map%3A_Organic_Chemistry_(Wade)_Complete_and_Semesters_I_and_II/Map%3A_Organic_Chemistry_(Wade)/19%3A_Ketones_and_Aldehydes/19.05%3A_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions_of_Ketones_and_Aldehydes" rel="internal"> Section 19.5</a>. One of the OH groups of the <em>gem</em>-diol is oxidized to create a carbonyl (C=O) thereby forming a carboxylic acid.</span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><span><img alt="reaction equation showing aldehyde conversion to gem-diol, followed by oxidation to carboxylic acid.svg" style="width: 416px; height: 78px;" width="416px" height="78px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387961/reaction_equation_showing_aldehyde_conversion_to_gem-diol%252C_followed_by_oxidation_to_carboxylic_acid.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=416&amp;height=78" /></span></p>

</div></div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="Oxidation_of_Ketones_and_Baeyer-Villiger_Oxidation"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-36383 editable">Oxidation of Ketones and&nbsp;Baeyer-Villiger Oxidation</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span>Because ketones do not have hydrogen atom attached to their carbonyl, they are resistant to oxidation. Only very strong oxidizing agents such as potassium manganate(VII) (potassium permanganate) solution oxidize ketones. However, this type of powerful oxidation occurs with cleavage, breaking carbon-carbon bonds and forming two carboxylic acids. Because of this destructive nature this reaction is rarely used.</span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><span><img alt="Oxidation of cyclopentanone to form pentanedioic acid.svg" style="width: 403px; height: 62px;" width="403px" height="62px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387962/Oxidation_of_cyclopentanone_to_form_pentanedioic_acid.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=403&amp;height=62" /></span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383">Oxidation of cyclopentanone to form pentanedioic acid</p>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span><strong>Peroxycarboxylic acids</strong>, such as <strong><span class="external">meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid (mCPBA)</span></strong>, are capable of oxidizing ketones to esters in a reaction known as the <strong>Baeyer-Villiger oxidation</strong>. Baeyer-Villiger oxidation has considerable synthetic utility because ketones normally are difficult to oxidize without degrading the structure to smaller fragments. </span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><img alt="kekule structure of a generic peroxycarboxylic acid and meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid.svg" style="width: 387px; height: 102px;" width="387px" height="102px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387963/kekule_structure_of_a_generic_peroxycarboxylic_acid_and_meta-chloroperoxybenzoic_acid.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=387&amp;height=102" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><span><img alt="generic reaction equation for oxidation of a ketone to an ester using a peroxyacid.svg" style="width: 259px; height: 95px;" width="259px" height="95px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387964/generic_reaction_equation_for_oxidation_of_a_ketone_to_an_ester_using_a_peroxyacid.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=259&amp;height=95" /></span></p>

<p class="lt-chem-36383">Baeyer-Villiger oxidations can be used with both straight chain ketones and cyclic ketones as shown in the following examples.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383"><img alt="Baeyer-Villiger Oxidation of 2-octanone to form hexyl ethanoate.svg" style="width: 444px; height: 50px;" width="444px" height="50px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387965/Baeyer-Villiger_Oxidation_of_2-octanone_to_form_hexyl_ethanoate.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=444&amp;height=50" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383">Baeyer-Villiger Oxidation of 2-octanone to form hexyl ethanoate</p>

<p align="center" class="lt-chem-36383" style="mso-margin-top-alt:auto;mso-margin-bottom-alt:
auto;text-align:center;line-height:normal"><span><img alt="Baeyer-Villiger Oxidation of cyclohexanone to 6-hexanolactone.svg" style="width: 262px; height: 76px;" width="262px" height="76px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387966/Baeyer-Villiger_Oxidation_of_cyclohexanone_to_6-hexanolactone.svg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=262&amp;height=76" /></span></p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36383">Baeyer-Villiger Oxidation of cyclohexanone to 6-hexanolactone</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="Mechanism_2"></span><h3 style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal;" class="lt-chem-36383 editable"><span><strong>Mechanism</strong></span></h3>

<p class="lt-chem-36383"><span>The mechanism of the Baeyer-Villiger oxidation has been studied extensively and is of interest because it involves a rearrangement step in which a substituent group </span>(R) moves from a carbon to an oxygen. In the first step, one oxygen from the peroxy carboxylic acid adds to the carbonyl group of the ketone. The adduct has multiple oxygen atoms on which protons can reside. An intramolecular proton transfer followed by protonation allows for generation of the Criegee intermediate. Migration of an alkyl group and elimination of a carboxylic acid, R<sup>1</sup>CO<sub>2</sub>H, then occur in the fourth step. This generates a protonated form of the ester product, which is deprotonated in the final step of the mechanism.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/19:_Aldehydes_and_Ketones-_Nucleophilic_Addition_Reactions/19.03:_Oxidation_of_Aldehydes_and_Ketones" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Step_1:_Nucleophilic_attack_on_the_carbonyl"></span><h4 style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal;" class="lt-chem-36383 editable"><span><strong>Step 1:</strong></span> Nucleophilic attack on the carbonyl</h4>

